        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "a349c3c9-b847-51ae-9c5e-79f54c3b3cc4",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Eardwulf of Northumbria, Æthelred I of Northumbria, Ælfwald II of Northumbria, Æthelwald Moll, Æthelthryth, Alhred\n- Places: Northumbria\n- Concepts: kingship, deposition, assassination attempt, dynastic strife, murder, reigns\n\nFacts:\n- Æthelred I of Northumbria was born around 762 and was killed on 18 April 796.\n- Æthelred I of Northumbria was the son of Æthelwald Moll and Æthelthryth.\n- Æthelred I of Northumbria possibly became king while still a child after Alhred was deposed.\n- Æthelred I of Northumbria ruled Northumbria from 774 to 779 and again from 790 until he was murdered in 796.\n- Northumbria in the last years of the eighth century was marked by dynastic strife between several noble families.\n- In 790, Æthelred I of Northumbria attempted to have Eardwulf of Northumbria assassinated.\n- According to one tradition, Ælfwald II of Northumbria reigned as king of Northumbria after Eardwulf of Northumbria was deposed in 806.\n- Eardwulf of Northumbria may have had a second reign from 808 until perhaps 811 or 830.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
